egin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=4\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99411/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99411"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99418/selection/e33d490ad853f9eb0a90f0a87460c250f4854ed7af7fa4912cde5959bfe07006" :answer/id #uuid "7e9b94bc-38f7-5d1b-97ff-9b91e9295eba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99418/selection/71c81fd070e542373eb337c2b8ef2aa3138a87b31e2b71dd9257fe95e9999c45" :answer/id #uuid "4f64cdbd-83d6-50f3-a8b1-1398ffd50f68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99418/selection/6ea6a0d7d9d84b19c4752bc52f0eee3730e68d38d15206068a5ceaea19019020" :answer/id #uuid "86e22acc-95ed-5062-bdaf-f132d09ec548" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99418/selection/f9f8335c52fdcc7962dd7715ea4deef43794c09000688bcaccf6643afbf42028" :answer/id #uuid "d9c96893-a284-5dd5-bfed-520039e923e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99418/selection/bae711a42a87c0cf483ba4e9918e9b139d24149201c7b8c99a800831f8061e7e" :answer/id #uuid "7881da0c-1113-5235-9f30-8e327293c472" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99418/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "37bbbc51-02da-57eb-9eca-78cee87f027a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99418/selection/bae711a42a87c0cf483ba4e9918e9b139d24149201c7b8c99a800831f8061e7e" :answer-field/choices ["q-99418/selection/e33d490ad853f9eb0a90f0a87460c250f4854ed7af7fa4912cde5959bfe07006" "q-99418/selection/71c81fd070e542373eb337c2b8ef2aa3138a87b31e2b71dd9257fe95e9999c45" "q-99418/selection/6ea6a0d7d9d84b19c4752bc52f0eee3730e68d38d15206068a5ceaea19019020" "q-99418/selection/f9f8335c52fdcc7962dd7715ea4deef43794c09000688bcaccf6643afbf42028" "q-99418/selection/bae711a42a87c0cf483ba4e9918e9b139d24149201c7b8c99a800831f8061e7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99418" :question/id #uuid "80370013-119d-5754-8de4-7031d8d553b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-99418" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2-3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-2+3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99418/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99418"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-38562/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "16c8f0ff-aa17-5c60-a304-6bf5aa38e859" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38562/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer/id #uuid "6292f2cb-4fb3-5c84-9b96-954834eb5063" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38562/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "f6715ecf-bc9c-5b17-b358-ad6ad73c5323" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38562/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "a9e5fe18-cf22-54e6-8735-52a678dd27b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38562/selection/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" :answer/id #uuid "08998747-7d4f-57fa-bb73-d4f6d50f1676" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38562/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8290e73-b03d-5954-ae27-64017cba770e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38562/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer-field/choices ["q-38562/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" "q-38562/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" "q-38562/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" "q-38562/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-38562/selection/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-38562" :question/id #uuid "5c1f1f01-a823-5b15-bbf5-e4ba72353ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-38562" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+2\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+2\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-4+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-4+2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38562/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38562"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99469/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b77f7641-3773-5a78-a934-785b53a1d0ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99469/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "a648820e-f3a5-5dba-a8e3-b6cffb9b816d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99469/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "dc818493-75f2-5752-bb47-ceb5835fa483" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99469/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "da621899-e44d-5bb6-940f-6e215e921e7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99469/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer/id #uuid "41cdc071-bd1e-5ac8-99b4-21a8aff7b272" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99469/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d41f6a72-5970-5cef-a749-881d72649e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99469/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-99469/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-99469/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" "q-99469/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" "q-99469/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-99469/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99469" :question/id #uuid "fb9302d7-9539-5a6b-969f-fec724275dd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-99469" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(2-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-99469/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99469"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99480/selection/94529360c5d2902297f79f4ead19c52f4b3fc0cf2d49e8939f76c80796234c59" :answer/id #uuid "af00d39d-220b-5038-b0d9-28954b511111" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99480/selection/3b526d0871203a7fa20c2a891525c650a26e23eb2a93fea36737a1cf4d4423af" :answer/id #uuid "9a6ce13d-afe4-5d19-ad1a-da2f57044c42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99480/selection/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer/id #uuid "a26fd9e0-d882-5271-a423-c0fc4ce4ce56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99480/selection/671723d87f1a67d9aa6a1f954b934e271d81eedfb75a65037fca3403ebebfd96" :answer/id #uuid "152a5a4b-e570-5685-a4f8-764ec14745c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99480/selection/e6d3118aceb74281e48cb90e485dc7fa200629b24db3905678cd82e9dabe1a76" :answer/id #uuid "eff3b69b-c309-5a26-8411-95439a3599ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99480/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e773762-00d2-5913-a740-949a1355328c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99480/selection/3b526d0871203a7fa20c2a891525c650a26e23eb2a93fea36737a1cf4d4423af" :answer-field/choices ["q-99480/selection/94529360c5d2902297f79f4ead19c52f4b3fc0cf2d49e8939f76c80796234c59" "q-99480/selection/3b526d0871203a7fa20c2a891525c650a26e23eb2a93fea36737a1cf4d4423af" "q-99480/selection/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" "q-99480/selection/671723d87f1a67d9aa6a1f954b934e271d81eedfb75a65037fca3403ebebfd96" "q-99480/selection/e6d3118aceb74281e48cb90e485dc7fa200629b24db3905678cd82e9dabe1a76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99480" :question/id #uuid "049b3a30-c66d-50f0-8523-4c22e52552d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99480" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1-2\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -13 & -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -13{x}_{1}+(-3+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-3-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-3-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{13}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-99480/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99480"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38565"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-2\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+3\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-39143/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "c4f2c36b-6142-5b8b-83e0-433ceedecb92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39143/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "5ce461d8-d8e1-55c1-92ef-deae216d72ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39143/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "3ca2c3bb-f761-531a-85b6-efb9dab85f58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39143/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "6611222f-9dae-5ebb-94b2-89c731520f5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39143/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "cdf6d7fc-3930-59e7-a472-8eb4d94d459e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39143/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd42e1d-6d56-5f3b-b165-61f58e870e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39143/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer-field/choices ["q-39143/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-39143/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" "q-39143/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" "q-39143/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-39143/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39143" :question/id #uuid "00edf80e-08fc-5c39-9cfb-f07f06d93758" :question/math-academy-id "q-39143" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3+2\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3+2\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -10{x}_{1}+(-6-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39143/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39143"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5531"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/d1bc2b703c1d73e735bdbb1d2abab86f86447e87fbeabde57126114726f78757.png)\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PCP^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/558cbc7aba862d44e34a7b109ea84c1f7a6f3f1f4b505e5f476e62a29fdd654b.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\n- From the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ca2243ed8015927b9db5428d676ee59907264fc223721246a81b2e73aca3b351.png) we deduce that $\\textrm{Re}(\\lambda)={\\color{blue}3}$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\lambda) = {\\color{red}-1}.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = {\\color{blue}3}+({\\color{red}-1})\\text{i} = 3-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/7a5bb57b71eb218271f2bb0ab718304d6b834738f1004eac7c501a7fd4ffa2b3.png) we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/0ea51255e7594236cdd5d8dee0997f64b49f77cbb77e8cfd97937830a862283b.png)\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ff8f2c8cace2b5c8c2c2ce2fcd0d5c24a4d1f4692e8e06464a9ae4666d429224.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5530"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/5f3cd6aeb514ee0a59d1f3534342f85e1c00743d9e7c7004b8aaf8ccfbe353f9.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1-4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/9fb15e52a1df912c9e5fb4238f45d705cd0a56fb7190620c0f0fd597f9a2b8c0.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/80bd36915851e6ebeb67befe8af41e5496fa5491b9d87c294d8b937eb586319f.png)\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8734"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/f4a051da70308d92a5530fca3ba9c214cff919a6072e0e4d863f5aad10f405ee.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2+4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/4a53548a17d9b4fd870ce70285bfc2c57cf6c8d352f75149e5aa7511ef4af853.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/aa5738b600440bf8327670fc21cd8005c660def232119b2b4808237d988367bd.png)\n\nwhile\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/fc91fcabd47720075061e670f62be9ea9b636070a05cba70752bb7060608b764.png)\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda.$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}},$ the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5532"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/74cc68e763183d4599dc223cce44849ace88129bbc82b6d49bcbafc4a1a8163d.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i},$ and that $A=PCP^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, find the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/b5c1766ea1d9eb7a913fd7be2d7bf71909c81da03aaff8f4c631e1a3955f9589.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/7f29c1c9be88268b14ef9131599420874fff6a399a3e3c92d3f4b635906163f6.png)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions to the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =-2+6\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+6\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3-6\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 9 & -3-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3-6\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+(-3-6\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3(1+2\\textrm{i})x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ab958a638d0bce3cb2498d6f64016e581bb303fd0df725abcfaa58dbd836b95d.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=3$, we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/631f718ed2642536c5a8e02dedc38544af5e8b6cc48bff7a0afcf77c0d057d1b.png)\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
